<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05YM">Remark <span data-tag="05YM">4.9.3.23</span>.</a> Let $\lambda $ be an uncountable cardinal. We say that $\lambda $ is <em>weakly inaccessible</em> if it is regular and satisfies the following: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(\ast ')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every cardinal $\kappa &lt; \lambda $, we also have $\kappa ^{+} &lt; \lambda $. That is, $\lambda $ is not a successor cardinal. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="05YL" href="/tag/05YL">4.9.3.22</a> that every strongly inaccessible cardinal is weakly inaccessible. Moreover, the converse follows from the generalized continuum hypothesis Remark <a data-tag="05YK" href="/tag/05YK">4.9.2.14</a>. </p>
</article>
